Functionality is the ability of the program to do something useful.
.NET is a language that is written with the notion of code reuse in mind. It is very functional in nature. For example, you may write a program that reads the text "Hello, World!" and prints "World!" to the console. The program "Reads a string and outputs a value" is functional. You can read a piece of text, convert it to a number, and then print that value. This is exactly what you get when you create a method called ReadLine().
The program I just wrote is not functional because it doesn't have the concept of reuse. I did not write this program with a thought in my mind that I would someday use this code. Instead, I wrote the method just because I could. When I later use the Readline method, all I get is an empty string. That is why I said that this is NOT a functional program.
I have seen some programmers try to make it more functional by using variables and functions. They write code such as the following:
 isTurtle() { var turtle = new Turtle(); }
In the above code, the variable turtle is assigned to, but it is still a variable. Now, this may be a good idea in a very small program, where only the code that needs to be changed is changed. But it does not work in larger programs. Consider this example:
 of Turtle { public static void Main(string[] args) { while (true) Console.WriteLine("Hello"); } }
 A more realistic example of a more complex program would be: of System.Console { static class Program { void main(String[] argv) {} } static Program t = { new Program(), "", "Turtles" }; } In this case, it would work, because the variables t and t.T are still variables, even though they are declared inside the main method. However, if you try writing this in Visual Basic, then you will get the error: Error: Cannot convert parameter 3 from 'T' to 'System.String'. of Program{static class System { int i = 0; public void Run() {}} } System t;
